the basic idea is simple:

Input: giving information to your program.
Output: getting information from your program.

for example: when a program asks for your name, and then displays the welcome message, it uses both input and output.

Code:

name=input("Enter your name:")
print("Welcome", name)

Output:

Enter your name: NamanNirwal
Welcome NamanNirwal

1. output in python: print()

the print() function displays information on the screen.

1.1 printing text

Code:

print("Hello World")
print("I am learning python")
print("Python is easy")

Output :

Hello World
I am learning python
Python is easy

Important point --> text must be enclosed in "Quotation marks".

1.2 Printing numbers

Important --> We dont need quotation marks when printing numbers.

Code:

print(10)
print(25.5)
print(10+20)

Output:

10
25.5
30

Important point --> Python  evaluates 10+20 first, then prints the result.

Compare these two languages:

Code:

print(10+20)
print("10+20")

Output:

30
10+20

Important --> The first performs a calculation. the second print literal text.

1.3 printing multiple values 

We can print multiple values in single statement.

Code:

name="Naman Nirwal"
age=22
print(name, age)

Output:

Naman Nirwal 22

VERY important --> by default, print separates multiple values with a space.

Code:

print("Python","Java","C++")

Output:

Python Java C++

1.4 printing variables 

A variable stores a value that you can display later.

Code:

name="NamanNirwal"
marks=90
percentage=85.5
print(name)
print(marks)
print(percentage)

